    {
      "id": "claim_e6eb328e6f3b4fceb65016390d1d37b4",
      "statement": "docker-compose-template.yaml defines three services on the app-network bridge: redis (image redis, restart always, ./data:/data volume, command redis-server --bind 0.0.0.0 --port 6379, port 6379 only exposed — not published to the host), redisinsight (redis/redisinsight published at 127.0.0.1:5540, depends_on redis, ./redisinsight:/data volume), and app (built from the Dockerfile as ghcr.io/ardoco/rest, published at 127.0.0.1:8080, depends_on redis).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docker-compose-template.yaml#L1-L42",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b63d7f4a2c2fbc2df5d37c34ec82b51d2bc29ecf6cb5711ee90adc5ae284109c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3816694f157f4c05b74fba9e36a44f86",
      "statement": "As shipped, the containerized Redis runs unauthenticated and the documented credential flow is inert: the compose template does not wire redis/redis.env into the redis service, spring.data.redis.username/password are empty in application.properties, and the spring.config.import of the .env file is commented out; redis_template.env (REDIS_PASSWORD, REDIS_USERNAME, REDIS_HOST, REDIS_PORT) documents the intended flow and README instructs renaming it to redis.env, so enabling credentials requires local adaptation.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://README.md#L9-L10",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0c865cd8b46588d646774915b7a2ee2a5a371a7f189ae4a6dfa89de87ac33f53:"
